// MOVING ABOARD THE SHARED SHIP, AND WHO HEARS ABOUT IT (increment 4 of
// docs/design/ship-homes-and-logistics.md, "getting around at ship scale"; read by src/ship/moves.rs,
// disk first, the copy built into the exe only when this file is missing or does not parse).
//
// The relay holds every player where their last accepted move left them. Each position update a
// game sends is checked against how far a person can really go in the time since
// (src/relay/handlers/move_check.rs). A move faster than that is answered with a CORRECTION: the
// game is told where the relay holds it and stands the player back there. It is never simply
// refused, because a game whose moves are all refused stands frozen on everyone else's screen.
//
// The moves that are fast but real are told apart, not allowed for by a bigger number:
//   - arriving (the welcome stands the player where the relay put them: their door, the
//     Commons, the spot a reconnect kept), Respawn and stepping out and back in (the relay spawns
//     them afresh), which the relay itself decides;
//   - a reconnect that finds the player still in the world: one move as far as they could have
//     walked while the connection was down, at most 90 m (`FAR_FROM_HELD_M`, src/ship/moves.rs);
//   - a teleporter or other transit link, named by its id (src/ship/transit.rs);
//   - shutting the build editor, which stands the player at their build spot on their own plot,
//     at standing height on its floor;
//   - driving a vehicle, at that vehicle's own speed (data/vehicles/kits.ron, `speed_mps`), when
//     that is faster than on foot.
//
// moving
//   on_foot_mps      The fastest a person goes on foot, metres a second across the floor, every
//                    boost included: the walk (5 m/s) times the sprint (1.9) times the speed
//                    buffs of data/status_effects.csv at their most stacks (Speed Boost 1.3 three
//                    times, Well Nourished 1.1) times the fastest boots (1.05): 24.1. A test
//                    (moves.rs `the_on_foot_limit_covers_the_fastest_legitimate_walk`) fails when
//                    a new buff or a faster walk passes it.
//   vertical_mps     The fastest up or down: a jump leaves at 5 m/s, a ladder climbs at 3 m/s, an
//                    elevator car rides slower still, and a fall from the Commons' 8 m ceiling,
//                    the tallest zone, lands at 12.5 m/s.
//   jitter_margin    How much faster than the limits above the allowance builds up, as a share
//                    (0.25 = a quarter): updates that set off together can arrive at uneven gaps.
//   banked_s         How many seconds of unused allowance a player keeps: updates held up by the
//                    network arrive in a burst, each one carrying the walk of the time it waited.
//   slack_m          A fixed allowance on every move, metres: rounding, and getting into a cab.
//   correction_gap_s The least time between two corrections to one player: updates already on
//                    their way when the first was sent are dropped quietly instead.
//   correction_resend_s
//                    When a player's updates still say they have not stood where they were sent
//                    this long after a correction, it is sent again.
//   jump_every_s     Declared jumps (a teleporter, shutting the build editor) come at most one
//                    every this many seconds over time. The relay cannot see a teleporter in
//                    anyone's home or an editor being open, so it limits how often anyone may say
//                    so. A person cannot do it faster: a teleporter works again only once they
//                    have stepped off every pad and 1.2 s have passed (engine/move_check.rs
//                    TELEPORT_REARM_S), and the editor has to be opened and shut.
//   jump_burst       How many declared jumps a player keeps in reserve, so two that the network
//                    delivers together both pass.
//
// delivery
//   in_view_m        Another player or crew member this close (metres, in the ship's frame) is
//                    in view: their moves are delivered, and the game draws them.
//   out_of_view_m    Farther than this, they go out of view: the game is told to stop drawing
//                    them and is sent nothing more about them until they are in view again. The
//                    gap between the two keeps someone walking along the edge from flickering in
//                    and out. Game messages only ever go to players in the shared world, never to
//                    a socket that is only chatting.
//
// Every number above is a game rule chosen for this increment from the movement code, not a
// sourced figure. On the first test ship every place was within 210 m of every other, so with
// these radii everyone aboard saw everyone. Since the twelve plots along First Street
// (2026-10-04) the ship is 1.2 km long and they matter: neighbours, 99 m apart, see each other,
// and the households at the street's far end are out of view of the Commons until they walk up
// it (src/relay/handlers/game_interest.rs, the shipped-ship test; the co-presence rig keeps its
// far places inside the view of the walker watching, copresence-judge.js farPlaceInView).
(
    moving: (
        on_foot_mps: 25.0,
        vertical_mps: 20.0,
        jitter_margin: 0.25,
        banked_s: 1.5,
        slack_m: 1.0,
        correction_gap_s: 0.5,
        correction_resend_s: 2.0,
        jump_every_s: 1.0,
        jump_burst: 2.0,
    ),
    delivery: (
        in_view_m: 250.0,
        out_of_view_m: 300.0,
    ),
)
